<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gobarau Minaret</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th–16th century</span> · Also known as Gobarau Mosque</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gobarau Minaret is a tall mud-brick tower in Katsina, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, the remnant of a mosque traditionally dated to the late 15th or early 16th century. Tradition links it to the first Muslim king of Katsina, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadu-korau">Muhammadu Korau</a>. The mosque served as a centre of Islamic teaching that drew scholars from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gobarau-minaret.jpg" alt="Gobarau Minaret" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Derric Evans · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gobarau_Minaret_Katsina.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
